{"records":[{"id":"w24t4trkv5","title":"A dataset on NFKB1/NFKB2 knockout Jurkat cells and primary CD4+ T cells generated by CRISPR/Cas9 mediated gene disruption. ","description":"Specific biological roles of non-canonical nuclear factor-kappa B (NFKB) signaling in human T cells remain unclear. Therefore, we genetically modified Jurkat leukemic cells using the clustered regularly interspaced palindromic repeat (CRISPR)/CRISPR-associated protein 9 technology to better understand the NFKB signaling mechanisms. IKAROS family zinc finger-2 levels were downregulated in NFKB2-knocked-out cells but upregulated in NFKB1-knocked-out cells. To further understand the biological roles of NFKB signaling in CD4+ T cells, NFKB1/NFKB2 loci of human primary CD4+ T cells were genetically edited using the CRISPR/CRISPR-associated protein 9 technology. Quantitative polymerase chain reaction and fluorescence-activated cell sorting revealed the similar phenotypes and gene expression profiles of NFKB1/NFKB2-knocked-out human CD4+ T cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-01-06T16:44:39.354Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w24t4trkv5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w24t4trkv5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yohei","last_name":"Sato","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Biotechnology","Genome Editing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6z6zd9gjbb","title":"RNA-seq data for a small molecule alleviating C9orf72 ALS pathology by interfacing G4C2 RNA repeats with endogenous decay pathways","description":"A hexanucleotide repeat expansion in intron 1 of the C9orf72 gene is the most common genetic cause of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and frontotemporal dementia, or c9ALS/FTD.  The RNA transcribed from the expansion, r(G4C2)exp, causes various disease-associated pathologies, including intron retention, aberrant translation that produces toxic dipeptide repeat proteins (DPRs), and sequestration of RNA-binding proteins (RBPs) within RNA foci in a subset of cells in the central nervous system.  Identification of this RNA species as the central toxin in c9ALS/FTD has provided a potential therapeutic target.  Here, we show a repurposed antitumor drug obtained through compound derivatization potently and selectively interacts with the disease-associated r(G4C2)exp and mitigates c9ALS/FTD disease pathologies in patient-derived spinal neurons. We performed the total RNA-seq of compound-treated cells from both ALS-patients and healthy donors to support that this small molecule has limited off targets on the human transcriptome.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-10-05T06:47:30.887Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6z6zd9gjbb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6z6zd9gjbb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Bush","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuquan","last_name":"Tong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Samantha","last_name":"Meyer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haruo","last_name":"Aikawa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Childs-Disney","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Matthew","last_name":"Disney","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ycnm3f8gsf","title":"Reconstitution of C9orf72 GGGGCC repeat-associated non-AUG translation with purified human translation factors","description":"Data in ''Reconstitution of C9orf72 GGGGCC repeat-associated non-AUG translation with purified human translation factors\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-08-31T14:22:46.543Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ycnm3f8gsf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ycnm3f8gsf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hayato","last_name":"Ito","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Luminescence","Western Blot","Gene Translation","In Vitro Study"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fm4h3mphcr","title":"NGS, confocal microscopy and flow cytometry data of CRISPR/Cas9-edited ARE1 and KAT2 genes in transgenic maize in vitro tissues exposed to chromatin modifiers","description":"This dataset contains raw NGS reads (NGS_data folder), fluorescence microscopy photos (Fig7B Microscopy Originals.lif) and flow cytometry data (@Fig6 FACS original data.zip) derived from transgenic maize (Zea mays L.) in vitro tissues exposed to chromatin modifiers. They are presented in research article ˝Variable Alterations in Frequencies of CRISPR/Cas9-Induced Nucleotide Deletion and Insertion in Transgenic Maize Callus Tissues Exposed to Chromatin Modifiers˝.\nStable transgenic maize lines harboring active CRISPR/Cas9 vector systems targeting either the abnormal cytokinin response 1 (ARE1) gene or the potassium channel gene (KAT2) were used. Mutagenic events following exposure to elevated temperatures (37 °C for 48 h) and various chromatin-modifying compounds were quantified. As a novel approach, the mutagenic potential of combining chromatin-modifying agents with heat exposure was examined.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-24T23:17:12.229Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fm4h3mphcr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fm4h3mphcr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bettina","last_name":"Nagy","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Györgyi","last_name":"Ferenc","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ferhan","last_name":"Ayaydin","orcid":"0000-0003-4450-0667"},{"first_name":"Divya","last_name":"Teja","orcid":"0009-0001-0210-7878"},{"first_name":"Dénes","last_name":"Dudits","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zoltán","last_name":"Zombori","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikolett","last_name":"Laszlo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aysha","last_name":"Jameel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gábor","last_name":"Horváth","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"EU’s Horizon 2020 Research and Innovation Program","grant_id":" 739593"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03g2am276","name":"National Research, Development and Innovation Office","grant_id":"RRF 2.3.1–21-2022- 00007 grant for the ‘National Laboratory Program of Agrobiotechnology and Precision Plant Breeding to Support Food Safety’"}],"categories":["Confocal Microscopy","Flow Cytometry","Next Generation Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nb9hh8yyds","title":"Cross-regulation between TDP-43 and paraspeckles promotes pluripotency-differentiation transition","description":"additional supplementary data:\n- Sanger sequencing and confirmatory gel electrophoresis confirming the CRISPR-Cas9 edited human and mouse embryonic stem cells presented in the manuscript under review.\n\n- additional supplementary data: raw unprocessed confocal images presented in Fig2 of the manuscript under review: ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-03-18T19:45:41.433Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nb9hh8yyds","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nb9hh8yyds"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Miha","last_name":"Modic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jernej","last_name":"Ule","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Micha","last_name":"Drukker","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Sequencing","Western Blot","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":145879,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":149,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":145879,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":145021,"restricted":71,"embargoed":787},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3479},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0